<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The complete set of files this app will ever hand to another app.

  This file is the whole attack surface of the provider, and the reason it is this
  short is deliberate. A <provider> is a path-traversal waiting for a caller: every
  element below widens the set of files a granted content:// URI can address, and
  every element omitted narrows it. The failure mode is not a crash, it is another
  app reading a file the user never chose to share.

  ONE ELEMENT, ON PURPOSE:

    <files-path name="out" path="out/" />

  app-private internal storage, one named subdirectory. The "out" directory is the
  only place the app writes an artifact it intends to hand outward (generated
  notes, exported model metadata, a trace the user asked to share).

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED, and why each omission is a security decision
  rather than a missing feature:

    <root-path/>          would expose the entire filesystem under this app's
                          access, including its private data directory. There is no
                          version of "share a file" that needs this.
    <external-path/>      sdcard and every other app's shared storage. Declaring
                          it would make a bug in a path-building call a data breach
                          of every file on the device, not just our own.
    <cache-path/>          cache is written speculatively and can hold partial
                          downloads and in-flight state. Sharing it turns a
                          temporary file into a durable disclosure.
    <files-path path="."/> the whole internal files directory: databases, shared
                          preferences, the session store. Everything a user typed
                          into the app lives there. We do not share our own history.
    <files-path/> (bare)   same as the above, and is the single most common way a
                          FileProvider is mis-scoped in the wild.

  If a future feature needs to share a file from outside files/out/, that feature
  earns its own <files-path> line here with a comment saying why, and nothing else
  changes. Scope grows one line at a time, on purpose, in review.

  ProviderPathScopeTest asserts these properties against this file on the JVM, so
  a future edit that widens the scope without the review conversation fails CI
  instead of shipping.
-->
<paths>
    <files-path
        name="out"
        path="out/" />
</paths>
